reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:3:LFM3 can plausibly match GUI-Owl-1.5-2B on this budget, but only against the right target and only by fixing one behaviour: answering questions. The fair target is GUI-Owl under our on-device budget (one screenshot, 4-bit weights, llama.cpp), where it scores **23/116**. Its self-reported **67.9** comes from a five-screenshot bf16 harness that costs 61 s per step on our Mac, and nothing reachable here can claim or beat that number. Against the matched number, the whole gap sits in AndroidWorld's **25 answer-scored tasks**: GUI-Owl answered **10** correctly, while LFM3 has never emitted an `answer` action and instead declares "complete" after navigating. On the other 91 tasks v1 already solves **15 to GUI-Owl's 13**. Published small-agent results and our own v2/v3 runs agree that the content of supervision (thoughts, answer-terminated trajectories, recovery data) and environment RL move AndroidWorld, while more static demonstrations do not. The next GPU hour should therefore train v4: v3 plus navigate-then-answer episodes in AndroidWorld's answer formats, with hyperparameters and image budget unchanged. Proving parity costs more than reaching it. A single 116-task run detects only gaps of about 14 tasks, and seed 30 has become a development set. A defensible "matches" therefore needs a pre-registered 8-task non-inferiority margin, at least three sealed held-out seeds (about 350 paired episodes), template-clustered tests, and GUI-Owl's best one-screenshot configuration as the comparator. All of this is paid for in emulator hours, not GPU hours.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:7:The comparison target has to be fixed before the method. GUI-Owl-1.5-2B reports **67.9% on AndroidWorld (AW)** using its authors' harness. That harness keeps the last five screenshots as multi-turn image messages, along with the model's own replies. It sends full-resolution PNGs to a bf16 vLLM server: up to about 3.07 MP, or roughly 2,500 visual tokens per 1080×2400 screen. It also forces a `terminate` on the step after an `answer` ([gui_owl.py](https://github.com/X-PLUG/MobileAgent/blob/main/Mobile-Agent-v3.5/android_world_v3.5/android_world/agents/gui_owl.py); [HF card](https://huggingface.co/mPLUG/GUI-Owl-1.5-2B-Instruct)). On our Mac that configuration costs **61 s per step at about 10.7k prompt tokens**, against 2.25–2.7 s for LFM3 ([GUI-Owl lessons](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/docs/GUI_OWL_LESSONS.md)).
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:9:The number we can actually compare against comes from running GUI-Owl under our deployment budget: its official prompt and action parser, one screenshot capped at about 1,024 visual tokens, Q4_K_M weights with a Q8 mmproj in llama.cpp, temperature 0, and AW's default step budget of 10× task complexity at seed 30. Under those settings it scores **23/116 (19.8%)** ([summary](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/androidworld/guiowl15_2b_matched.summary.json)). Precision is matched, because LFM3's AW runs use the same Q4_K_M plus Q8_0-mmproj artifacts ([eval protocol](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/docs/EVAL_PROTOCOL.md)). If anything, GUI-Owl got the richer input: up to 1,024 visual tokens against LFM3's ~720, and 2,261 mean prompt tokens against 1,145.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:11:The 48-point drop from 67.9 is a configuration loss, not a measurement bug. Our step budget is the official one, and faithful third-party reruns of the 32B sibling land within half a point of its published score (**69.3 ± 0.5** over three seeds) ([Beyond the GUI Paradigm](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.19388)). The authors' own ablation attributes only about 2–3 points to history images, measured on GUI-Owl-7B on OSWorld ([Mobile-Agent-v3](https://arxiv.org/html/2508.15144v2)), whereas serving-stack differences alone cost GUI-Owl-7B 30–45 AW points in user reports, where the maintainer's first question was whether the user had quantized the model ([issue #203](https://github.com/X-PLUG/MobileAgent/issues/203)). This collapse under on-device budgets is general, not specific to GUI-Owl. MAI-UI-2B reports 49.1 in its own harness, but on a phone NPU at Q4_0 with 512×512 input it scored **8.2% on AW's easy split and 0% on medium plus hard** ([DroidPrefill](https://arxiv.org/html/2611.20772)). The matched comparison is therefore the product-relevant one. It must, however, carry the label "GUI-Owl-1.5-2B under a one-screenshot, 4-bit budget."
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:13:Within the matched run, the gap is concentrated almost entirely in one task type. In our AW copy, **25 of the 116 tasks are scored on the agent's answer text**, which the information-retrieval checkers read from `interaction_cache` ([information_retrieval.py](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/android_world/android_world/task_evals/information_retrieval/information_retrieval.py)). GUI-SD-v2 counts 27 under a broader definition ([GUI-SD-v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.27307)). The table splits the seed-30 results by task type and by how each episode ended ([GUI-Owl failure modes](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/androidworld/guiowl15_2b_matched.failure_modes.json); [v1 failure modes](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/androidworld/lfm3vl2b_ft.failure_modes.json)).
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:26:On action tasks the two models are level: 8 tasks were solved only by v1 and 6 only by GUI-Owl, a difference within noise. On answer tasks the gap is complete, and it is behavioural. GUI-Owl solved 10 answer tasks; on 8 of those, v1 navigated for 3–8 plausible steps and then emitted `status complete`. In SimpleCalendarEventsOnDate it opened Simple Calendar, tapped October 24 and declared completion. In NotesMeetingAttendeeCount it searched for the meeting title, opened the result and stopped ([v1 trajectories](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/androidworld/lfm3vl2b_ft/run_20261117T030335477504)).
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:30:AW's scoring rule explains why GUI-Owl's caution pays only on answer tasks. AW scores an episode `task_successful if done else 0` ([suite_utils.py](https://github.com/google-research/android_world/blob/main/android_world/suite_utils.py)), so a false `complete` and an exhausted budget both score zero. GUI-Owl's calibrated termination therefore earns points only where it ends with a correct answer or a correct `terminate`. On action tasks, LFM3's 45 false completes cost it nothing that GUI-Owl's 92 budget exhaustions did not also cost GUI-Owl. To match GUI-Owl under this budget, LFM3 does not need to navigate better. It needs to end question goals with a correct, correctly formatted answer.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:44:AW did not move with them. Of the first 72 v3 episodes, 8 solved; paired with v1 on 70 tasks, each model won 2 tasks the other lost (p = 1.0). v3 also produced zero answer actions. A recheck at 104 finished episodes found 12 successes and still no answer action, and **12 of its 19 finished answer-scored episodes ended in `status complete`** ([v3 run](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/androidworld/lfm3vl2b_v3/run_20261117T210853476910)).
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:50:These results correct the project's earlier training-methods report on five points. First, **exhausting the step budget is not free**: under the done-flag rule it scores zero even when the final device state is correct. Second, **GUI-Owl does terminate its own episodes**, ending 24 itself with 23 correct; the earlier "never terminates" claim came from a summarizer that recognized only our own `status` action, which commit bebf929 fixed. Third, **GUI-Libra's 3B gain came mostly from data mixing**: mixing in reasoning-free copies of the data was worth **+6.1**, while action-token reweighting added only **+1.7** ([GUI-Libra](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22190)). Fourth, **LFM3-VL-2B does not use a SigLIP2 tower**; its vision encoder is an in-house 164M convolution-plus-attention model ([Liquid LFM3](https://www.liquid.ai/blog/lfm3)). Fifth, **adding a history screenshot is no longer a path to parity**, because under a matched-input rule every image added to LFM3 must also go to GUI-Owl, which was trained to use multi-image history.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:54:GUI-Owl-1.5 is a full post-train of Qwen3-VL in three stages ([Mobile-Agent-v3.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2602.16855v1)). Continued pre-training on UI, knowledge, world-modelling and tool data comes first. SFT follows, on trajectories from a "hybrid data flywheel": human-authored task graphs per app, cloud-phone rollouts truncated at the last verified checkpoint, human demonstrations for tasks that still failed, and web-rendered "vibe-coded" virtual environments, with every step carrying a teacher-written thought that fuses observation, memory, reflection and progress. The last stage is MRPO, an online-RL method in the GRPO family that oversamples rollouts and swaps in opposite outcomes so groups never collapse to all-success or all-failure, training on one device family at a time. The ablations that exist are all at 7–8B. The unified thoughts are worth **+6.6 AW points** (65.0 → 71.6 at 8B-Thinking). In the v1 report, offline reasoning synthesis added about 18 points and three rounds of online rejection sampling about 16 ([Mobile-Agent-v3](https://arxiv.org/html/2508.15144v2)). Virtual-environment trajectories moved the in-house mobile evaluation from **50.0% to 86.7%**.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:56:Much is undisclosed. No training data, hyperparameters or 2B ablations are released. The papers do not say whether AW apps or tasks were excluded, and v1 openly used AW as its development metric. A maintainer has said the Mobile-Agent-v3 framework incorporates "some context-sensitive prompts from AndroidWorld" ([issue #161](https://github.com/X-PLUG/MobileAgent/issues/161)). No third party has reproduced the 2B's AW score, although its grounding scores reproduce within about 1.5 points ([ClawGUI](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.11784)).
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:64:| MAI-UI-2B ([paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2512.22047)) | Qwen3-VL-2B | self-evolving SFT + online GRPO | current + 2 previous screenshots | 49.1 | SFT 45.1 → RL 49.1 |
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:68:| GUI-Libra-3B ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22190)) | Qwen2.5-VL-3B | SFT on 81K AC/Odyssey/AMEX steps re-annotated with reasoning → offline GRPO | screenshot + action history | 25.2 | SFT 5.2 → mixed 11.3 → reweighted 13.0 → GRPO 25.2 |
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:76:Several headline numbers do not compare cleanly with a strict no-AW rule. UI-Voyager trains on AW's own templates, AutoPlay generates its tasks on the 20 AW apps ([AutoPlay](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.25047)), and ADMIRE runs RL inside the AW sandbox ([ADMIRE](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.11524)). MAI-UI's prompt hard-codes AW app names ([prompt.py](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Tongyi-MAI/MAI-UI/main/MAI-UI/src/prompt.py)), and Step-GUI-4B's headline 75.8 is pass@3, against a pass@1 of 63.9 ([Step-GUI](https://arxiv.org/html/2512.15431)). Same-app training inflates AW, too. In AndroidWorld-Generalization, RL gains shrink from +26.1 on unseen instances to +15.7 on unseen templates and +8.3 on unseen apps ([AW-Generalization](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07432)). Our rule forbids AW tasks, AW variants, AW evaluation trajectories and AW checkers as rewards. That is stricter than most of the field and stricter than anything GUI-Owl discloses.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:88:Volume of the same kind of data stops helping online, while app coverage keeps helping. In PhoneWorld, adding 0 to 36K mock-app steps to a 9B model raised in-domain success from 14.2 to 73.3, while **AW stayed near 46%**. At a fixed 10K steps, broadening from 5 to 34 apps moved **AW from 61.2 to 71.6** ([PhoneWorld](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.29486)).
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:94:Online RL produced the biggest measured jumps at 2–4B: **+14.5 for UI-Venus-1.5-2B**, **+16.9 in 50 steps for PhoneBuddy-4B**, and +4.0 for MAI-UI-2B. The smallest documented setup, though, used 64 parallel Docker emulators on 8×A6000 GPUs to move ClawGUI-2B on MobileWorld from 11.1 to 17.1 ([ClawGUI](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.11784)).
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:96:Offline GRPO with a small KL penalty is the strongest offline lever. It took GUI-Libra-3B **from 13.0 to 25.2 at KL 1e-3**, against 21.7 at KL 0 and 20.0 at KL 5e-2. The result carries three caveats: it started from a weak long-CoT SFT model, it used 81K curated steps, and Ferret gained only about 3 points from RL. No ≤4B paper reports LoRA-based GRPO on AW.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:98:Token-level on-policy distillation is closed to us. It helped LiteGUI-2B on desktop (OSWorld 6.04 → 9.32, where plain SFT dropped it to 3.25) ([LiteGUI](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07505)). It needs a teacher that shares the student's tokenizer, though, and LFM3-VL has no larger sibling. Only self-distillation or sequence-level teacher data remain available.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:102:Our setup differs from nearly every 2026 small-agent recipe on several axes:
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:113:No ≤4B paper ablates any of these axes on AW. The nearest evidence is from a robotics VLA, where LoRA r32 with a fully trained vision encoder matched full fine-tuning and freezing the vision side hurt ([LoRA for VLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.10172)). Under-training is not obviously harmful if RL follows. Ferret's RL gains were largest after short SFT, and in WinDOM an under-saturated SFT model gave GRPO a better starting point ([WinDOM](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.25964)).
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:119:Extra history screenshots help any model trained to use them. GUI-Owl-7B gained about 2.5 OSWorld points from one to three history images. A frontier model on the GMA benchmark gained 6 points from one to two images, and lost 7.7 when its previous thoughts were dropped ([GMA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.27477)). Under a matched-input rule that gain goes to GUI-Owl as well, so adding images probably narrows nothing.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:137:| More static human demonstrations | PhoneWorld-9B; our v2/v3 | AW flat | ~0 | — | stop |
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:139:| Offline GRPO, KL 1e-3 | GUI-Libra-3B; Ferret-3B | 13.0 → 25.2; ~25 → 28 | 0 to +5 | new tooling | only with confirmed Modal and a full smoke test |
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:140:| Online RL | UI-Venus-2B; ClawGUI-2B | +14.5; +6 MobileWorld at 64 envs | — | ≥64 environments | out of reach |
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:150:A fair claim needs three arms on one pinned AW commit, AVD and seed list. The matched one-screenshot GUI-Owl arm already run is the fair comparator. LFM3 runs under exactly the same rules. A GUI-Owl native-configuration reference is always shown beside the matched number, never in place of it. One screenshot is not alien to GUI-Owl. Its v1 harness used one screenshot plus text conclusions ([Mobile-Agent-v3](https://arxiv.org/html/2508.15144v2)), and GUI-Owl-1.5's implementation in the MobileWorld repository defaults to `history_n: 1` ([MobileWorld gui_owl_1_5.py](https://github.com/Tongyi-MAI/MobileWorld/blob/main/src/mobile_world/agents/implementations/gui_owl_1_5.py)). Fairness still requires equal tuning effort: try that MobileWorld-style prompt on the development seed and keep whichever variant scores better. Every input change made for LFM3, such as an installed-app list, more images or more pixels, must be mirrored in the GUI-Owl arm.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:156:Every arm must record the same pinned protocol ([eval protocol](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/docs/EVAL_PROTOCOL.md)): AW commit 55ddaf5 with our local patches, a Pixel 6 API 33 **arm64** image (most papers use x86_64, and the effect of CPU architecture is unmeasured), the 10× task-complexity step budget with the done-flag scoring rule, temperature 0 with a one-retry parse policy, and the emulator ID for each episode. Exceptions should be scored two ways. The primary score counts them as failures over 116. The secondary score follows official AW code, which turns an exception into NaN and drops the episode, scoring over completed episodes only; by that rule v1 is 15/114 ([suite_utils.py](https://github.com/google-research/android_world/blob/main/android_world/suite_utils.py)). Infrastructure crashes are re-run under a rule written down in advance.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:186:One held-out benchmark that neither model targeted would strengthen the claim. Candidates are AndroidLab's five non-AW apps or MobileWorld's GUI-only tasks, preferring deterministic checkers ([AndroidLab](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.24024); [AI Agents That Matter](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01502)). Whether AndroidLab's AVDs run on an arm64 Mac is untested, and MobileWorld needs a rooted Docker-in-Docker setup.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:192:v3 trained 1,250 steps of 32 rows (0.51 epoch of 78,952 rows) in **4,400 s on a Modal A100-40GB**, peaking at 11 GB of memory ([v3 training summary](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/training/lfm3vl2b_v3/train_summary.json)). With AndroidControl and development evaluations added, one v3-sized cycle takes about 1.5 GPU-hours, most of the ~2.5 G4-hours left on Colab. With $18.44 already spent on Modal this month, roughly one more run remains there if the earlier $26–28 ceiling still applies; treat that as unconfirmed. Emulator hours are free but slow, and they are the real constraint on fair evaluation. The plan below keeps hyperparameters, image budget and harness fixed for the training run, so that any change in AW can be attributed to data. Under the project's preflight rule, every paid step first runs end-to-end on a tiny limit, upload included.
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:196:| 0 (now) | Write the pre-registration. When v3's pass ends, run matched GUI-Owl on held-out seeds (e.g. 7, 1234, 2026) with results sealed. Test GUI-Owl's MobileWorld-style one-image prompt on seed 30. Package-screen the SFT data against AW's apps. | 0 | ~36 emulator-h | — |
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:198:| 2 | Train v4 with v3's exact hyperparameters; AndroidControl test, development splits and probes | ~1.5 G4-h | — | answer exact match on held-out question episodes clearly above 0; false-answer rate on action goals ≤ a few %; AndroidControl within ~2 points of v3 |
reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md:211:The second run depends on what step 3 reveals. If answers are emitted but mis-formatted, or the model answers on action goals, spend it on a corrected v4.1. If failures look like compounding errors, spend it on the earlier plan's on-policy correction round: roll v4 through about 1,500 AndroidControl and GUI-Odyssey train episodes on the Mac with its own history, and record first-divergence states with the gold label. At 7B, training on own-history states moved AW from 21.7 to 34.0 ([UI-S1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11543)), and keeping learner errors in the history beat pure expert distillation, 48.3 to 44.8 ([OpenMobile](https://arxiv.org/html/2604.15093)). Offline GRPO waits for a confirmed Modal budget and a pipeline that has already run end-to-end. Equally clear is what not to do: add more static human demonstrations, add history screenshots for parity, merge adapters, change the step budget or headline pass@k, fold harness tricks into model claims, or train the headline model on AW-app synthetic sets.
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:5:**Recommendation: finish a corrected, balanced v4 SFT run, then allocate the extra budget to one conservative static-state GRPO pilot with KL, initialized from v4.** Use the existing verified training states, one screenshot, short completions, language-only LoRA, and native Hugging Face generation. Give compatibility and throughput testing 45 minutes; if it fails, switch to targeted Qwen3-VL-8B sequence distillation plus a short SFT continuation. This choice combines actual small-model RL evidence with an unusually relevant implementation fact: Liquid publishes an LFM3-VL-2B LoRA GRPO notebook pinned to **TRL 1.16.0 and Transformers 5.20.2**. It establishes a concrete starting point, not demonstrated AndroidWorld improvement or a measured G4 runtime. [Liquid notebook](https://github.com/Liquid4All/cookbook/blob/main/finetuning/notebooks/lfm3_vl_grpo_with_trl.ipynb)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:11:| **1. Static-state GRPO/RLVR with KL after v4** | Best evidence-supported opportunity for an additional gain; size is unknown and regression is possible. **UI-Venus-2B: 39.0→45.3 AW, +6.3 percentage points** from offline RL. **GUI-Libra-3B: 13.0→25.2, +12.2 pp** after ASFT with KL=.001; KL=0 gives 21.7. These are different models, data, precision, and harnesses. [Venus Table 7](https://arxiv.org/html/2602.09082v1), [Libra Table 9](https://arxiv.org/html/2602.22190v1) | **8 h envelope; ≤4.75 h main optimization.** Libra's disclosed **4B** RL cost is 8 B200×16 h=128 B200 GPU-h; 3B hours are not disclosed. [Libra Appendix B](https://arxiv.org/html/2602.22190v1) | Conditional yes: vendor model-specific TRL example; no emulator needed. | Incomplete single-action rewards, flat reward groups, generation cost. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:14:| **4. Import GUI-Libra reasoning / GUI-Reflection recovery** | Useful research directions, weaker marginal fit tonight. Libra-3B's mixed reasoning/direct-action supervision changes AW **5.2→11.3**, then ASFT→13.0. GUI-Reflection's final AW **34.5 is at 8B**, not ≤4B. [Libra](https://arxiv.org/html/2602.22190v1), [Reflection](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.08012v1) | A bounded adapted subset could fit **2–4 h**, an engineering estimate excluding open-ended provenance repair. Prior local joins already found defects and overlap. [dataset audit](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/codex/dataset_viability_v4.md) | Medium for carefully joined labels; low for wholesale import. | Duplicate states, conflicting goals, multi-image dependence, wrong action mapping. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:16:| **6. Cross-model on-policy logit distillation / self-distillation** | Positive **2B desktop** evidence: LiteGUI OSWorld 6.04→9.32 with OPD, →11.56 with guided OPD. This is not AW evidence. GUI-SD-v2's AW evidence is **8B**, not ≤4B. [LiteGUI Table 4](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.07505v1), [GUI-SD-v2](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.27307v1) | LiteGUI's 2B GKD uses **8 A100s**, subsequent RL **16 A100s**, with a 32B teacher/judge; wall hours unspecified. [Appendix A](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.07505v1) | Poor for an overnight cross-architecture port. | Tokenizer/logit alignment, teacher memory, rollout synchronization, long contexts. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:32:| VeriGUI, 3B | With 30% synthetic recovery examples, recovery SR **29.7→45.5**, while ordinary step SR **42.6→41.7**; adding GRPO reaches 51.1 recovery SR / 46.6 step SR. These are offline/pseudo-online metrics. Training is **8 A100-80GB×(12 h SFT+20 h RL)**. [paper, ablations and Appendix D](https://arxiv.org/html/2604.05477v1) | Recovery data can trade normal accuracy for robustness. Do not present +15.8 recovery points as AW improvement or blindly copy a 30% mix. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:34:| MobileGym, 4B | Qwen3-VL-4B improves **9.4→22.2 on MobileGym**, not AndroidWorld. The disclosed training uses **3 GPUs and 96 environments**, group 8. [paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.26114v1) | Browser simulators avoid Android-emulator dependence, but are not a ready one-GPU overnight replacement. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:36:| LiteGUI, 2B | Naive GRPO after guided OPD **reduces OSWorld 11.56→6.56**; multi-solution rewards recover 11.84 and the full reward reaches 13.24. [Table 4](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.07505v1) | A valid alternative action must not be treated as wrong just because one demonstration chose something else. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:94:OpenMobile-v2 merits a more precise distinction than “all OpenMobile data are forbidden”: its simulator GUI subset is separable and its demonstrations are generated by Gemini-3.1-Pro-Preview, not GUI-Owl. However, the emulator inventory includes AndroidWorld support apps, the published mixture is not simulator-only, and its embedding-overlap analysis is not proof of task/checker independence. Treat simulator admission as a separate provenance review; the inspected data release also uses up to three screenshots. [paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.14157v1), [release schema](https://huggingface.co/datasets/OpenMobile-2/OpenMobile-Data-v2/raw/main/README.md) The project's code documents a relaxed brightness checker; do not import that evaluator into the matched comparison. [OpenMobile code README](https://github.com/OpenMobile2/OpenMobile2-Code/blob/main/README.md)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:98:Teacher-generated short response strings can be retokenized by LFM3 for ordinary SFT. Token-level Qwen→LFM KL needs an explicit alignment method; sharing a parameter count or using `trust_remote_code` does not align vocabularies. LiteGUI uses same-family Qwen teacher/student token distributions, while standard GKD documentation is not an LFM3-VL cross-tokenizer compatibility guarantee. Treat that port as unverified, not impossible. Self-distillation avoids the cross-model vocabulary issue but still needs informative privileges and validated VLM training. [LiteGUI method](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.07505v1), [TRL GKD](https://huggingface.co/docs/trl/en/gkd_trainer)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:106:| **TRL 1.16.0 + Transformers 5.20.2 + PEFT** | Liquid's notebook loads `AutoModelForImageTextToText` / `AutoProcessor` with remote code, uses language LoRA r16/alpha32, and passes the model object and processor to `GRPOTrainer`. [notebook](https://github.com/Liquid4All/cookbook/blob/main/finetuning/notebooks/lfm3_vl_grpo_with_trl.ipynb) | **Use this starting point.** This is source-level compatibility evidence; our GUI batch still needs a real forward/backward/save/reload smoke test. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:107:| TRL native generation | Pinned trainer has a VLM path and multimodal reference-log-prob handling. `use_transformers_continuous_batching=True` explicitly rejects multimodal processors. [v1.16.0 trainer](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/trl/trainer/grpo_trainer.py) | `use_vllm=False`, `use_transformers_continuous_batching=False`. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:109:| verl | Official docs support multimodal RL and PEFT LoRA generally, including merging adapters before rollout weight transfer. They explicitly require checking the training model, rollout engine, synchronization, and log-prob consistency separately. [VLM example](https://verl.readthedocs.io/en/latest/examples/multi_modal_example.html), [LoRA guide](https://verl.readthedocs.io/en/latest/advance/ppo_lora.html), [model extension guide](https://verl.readthedocs.io/en/latest/advance/fsdp_extension.html) | LFM3-VL end-to-end compatibility was **not verified**. No overnight port. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:112:The vendor notebook pins `torch==2.14.1`, `torchvision==0.29.1`, `torchao==0.18.0`, `trl==1.16.0`, `transformers==5.20.2`, `peft==0.21.2`, `accelerate==1.15.0`, `datasets==5.1.0`, `huggingface-hub==2.3.0`, `tokenizers==0.23.3`, and `safetensors==0.8.0`. Preserve the working v4 environment; create a separate environment only if necessary, record the actual CUDA/PyTorch build, and require import plus generation/backprop tests. Version agreement is not proof that a wheel supports the assigned GPU. [vendor notebook](https://github.com/Liquid4All/cookbook/blob/main/finetuning/notebooks/lfm3_vl_grpo_with_trl.ipynb)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:119:| Model-specific GRPO smoke and throughput test | 0.75 | At least two updates plus save/reload, then up to ten timed updates on realistic images. If not working within 45 minutes, choose fallback. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:120:| Main static GRPO | 4.75 | Up to 200 updates, reduced to fit measured throughput; checkpoint every 25. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:130:Calibrate on 128 training prompts with four sampled responses each. Favor states with mixed correct/incorrect outcomes; reserve roughly 20% for representative anchors. If fewer than about 10% of groups have useful reward variation after one bounded resampling, stop and use targeted SFT. All-zero answer groups need correct demonstrations before sparse RL can help; all-correct groups provide no centered reward advantage. This follows directly from within-group reward normalization, and the thresholds are my proposed stop rules. [TRL GRPO objective](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/docs/source/grpo_trainer.md)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:140:**Step 3 — Initialize a small adapter over the merged v4 checkpoint.** Merge v4 first, freeze it as the reference/base, and attach a fresh r16/alpha32 language-only adapter with no projector or vision training. This makes “adapter disabled” mean **v4**, not the original untuned model. TRL 1.16 also supports an initial reference copy when retraining an existing adapter; the merged-base route simply makes this experiment easier to audit. Verify identical policy/reference logits before the first update. [reference handling](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/trl/trainer/grpo_trainer.py)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:142:Use this proposed configuration, adapted from the vendor path and pinned TRL API. `chosen_steps` is computed from the smoke timing, at most 200; `run_dir` must be a new output directory. The learning rate is intentionally lower than the vendor's non-GUI demo. These are experimental settings, not validated LFM3 AndroidWorld optima. [vendor example](https://github.com/Liquid4All/cookbook/blob/main/finetuning/notebooks/lfm3_vl_grpo_with_trl.ipynb), [GRPOConfig v1.16.0](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/trl/trainer/grpo_config.py)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:152:cfg = GRPOConfig(
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:176:# Pass model object, processing_class=processor and peft_config to GRPOTrainer.
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:179:Keep the known v4 image preprocessing and history limit. Preflight tokenized prompt lengths; reject pathological examples rather than truncating away image markers or silently changing screenshot detail. Confirm that exactly one image reaches both policy and reference forwards. Log actual trainable parameter names and confirm zero vision/projector gradients. TRL defaults `beta` to zero, so explicitly setting it matters. `dapo` is the pinned library's default loss; this proposal does not reproduce Libra's complete objective or SNGS. [pinned configuration](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/trl/trainer/grpo_config.py), [local image handling](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/train/sft_vlm.py:128)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:181:**Step 4 — Measure, then commit the remaining hours.** Proposed 200 updates mean **1,600 distinct-prompt visits and 6,400 sampled completions**, at most 1,228,800 output tokens at the 192-token cap. These are counts of visits, not necessarily 1,600 unique states. Ten timed updates must include generation, reference scoring, optimizer work, and a representative save; SFT's 4.5 s/update is not a GRPO speed estimate. Under this exact batch configuration, 30/60/90/120 s per update implies 1.67/3.33/5.00/6.67 h for 200 updates. Choose `min(200, floor(remaining_main_seconds / measured_conservative_step_seconds))`, allowing for save overhead. These are arithmetic budget bounds, not measured performance.
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:183:If memory fails, make one bounded attempt with train microbatch 2, accumulation 16, and `generation_batch_size=16`; do not also set `steps_per_generation`. This keeps 32 completions per optimizer update while reducing generation pressure. Verify the resulting counters and time again. If it still fails, use the fallback. Do not introduce quantized training, flash-attention compilation, or a vLLM port during this retry. The generation-batch controls and divisibility requirements are documented in the pinned configuration. [GRPOConfig](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/trl/trainer/grpo_config.py)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:185:At 25-update intervals, record reward by stratum, zero-variance group fraction, entropy, KL, output length, truncation fraction, malformed actions, and deterministic false-answer rate. Suggested early stops: non-finite values; persistent >5% truncation; >3% false answers on held-out action terminals; or reward rising while held-out typed answer accuracy falls. These are proposal-specific safeguards. Do not add a length reward that encourages omitting evidence or truncating answers; capped but truncated outputs remain failures in behavioral evaluation even when excluded from the RL loss.
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:187:**Step 5 — Select without touching held-out AW.** Evaluate frozen v4 and the selected RL checkpoint on the accepted held-out QA episodes, paired original-goal terminal controls, and a fixed navigation sample from original validation. Report denominators after teacher rejection; the nominal 200 candidates are not necessarily 200 accepted labels. Measure exact typed answer correctness, premature `answer`, false `complete`, schema validity, and action-plus-argument correctness. Do not use teacher-forced loss or the earlier permissive ScreenQA F1 threshold as a substitute for answer correctness. [v4 development splits](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/data/v4_build_parquet.py), [metric caveat in v3 audit](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/codex/codex_v3_audit.md)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:189:My proposed RL promotion rule is: beat v4's answer exact-match on the fixed offline set, retain the ≤3% action-terminal false-answer bound, and avoid a material navigation regression. For a concrete preliminary screen, use a ≥5 pp answer improvement and ≤2 pp navigation drop; report uncertainty because a small set can make these thresholds noisy. Then apply the **existing** preregistered seed-30 gate: at least three answer-task successes and at least ten action-task successes (v3's 13 minus three). An offline pass is not an AW pass. Name this checkpoint `v4-RL` and record the new training/selection branch as a dated deviation before selection and before unsealing, rather than silently replacing the preregistered v4 arm. [preregistration](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/docs/PREREG_v4.md)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:195:**Fallback if the GRPO gate fails: targeted sequence distillation, not indiscriminate extra epochs.** Freeze the original v4. From training-only states, take at most 1,500 student failures or low-confidence cases: approximately 500 terminal questions, 500 navigation/goal-understanding states, and 500 completion/recovery controls. Ask Qwen8B for a brief rationale and one corrected response; for existing navigation labels retain verified gold actions and reject contradictory rewrites. Use actual observed recovery states if available; do not invent the effect of a wrong click. Validate labels with the strict typed checks above and a visual sample. This is a proposed use of the already operating teacher path. [teacher implementation](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/data/vm_v4_teacher.py)
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:210:| GRPO cannot find a positive answer | Within-group identical rewards yield no task advantage. [TRL docs](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/docs/source/grpo_trainer.md) | Mixed-outcome calibration; switch to demonstrations if sparse reward is uninformative. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:211:| OOM or generation dominates | Training microbatch and generation batch are different controls. [GRPOConfig](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/trl/trainer/grpo_config.py) | Short outputs, one image, teacher unloaded; one batch reduction then fallback. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:212:| VLM support inferred from text-model support | Continuous batching rejects VLM processors; vLLM inference support does not establish native adapter support. [TRL code](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/trl/trainer/grpo_trainer.py), [vLLM matrix](https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/models/supported_models/) | Follow the vendor's native-generation path; 45-minute gate. |
results/codex/codex_overnight_research.md:222:The required local reading comprised [fair-comparison report](</Users/alpha/lfm3-android/reports/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly.md>), [small-agent methods report](</Users/alpha/lfm3-android/reports/Small GUI agent training methods.md>), [STATUS](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/STATUS.md), [preregistration](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/docs/PREREG_v4.md), [v3 audit](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/codex/codex_v3_audit.md), and the six notes under [research_notes/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly](</Users/alpha/lfm3-android/research_notes/Beating GUI Owl at 2B fairly>): `data_and_environments.md`, `fair_evaluation.md`, `gui_owl_recipe.md`, `input_inference_harness.md`, `peer_small_models.md`, and `training_tunables.md`. Supporting local sources are linked at the claims above. In particular, this report does **not** adopt the older note's unmeasured suggestion that a GUI RL run will fit in about one GPU-hour.
